From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 02:40:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: msm8998-google-taimen: pull the NFC IRQ
 line down

gpio92 carries the PN553's interrupt and is described IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH.
Biasing it with a pull-up asserts that interrupt permanently whenever the
controller is not driving the line, which on mainline is most of the time:
nxp-nci holds VEN low until userspace calls dev_up(), while the irq is
requested at probe.

The result is a spurious interrupt that never goes away.  The handler's
first I2C read NACKs (-EREMOTEIO, "NFC: Read failed with error -121" at
t=10s every boot), the driver latches hard_fault and from then on returns
IRQ_HANDLED without touching the chip, so nothing ever deasserts the line.
Measured on a Pixel 2 XL: 26,176,487 interrupts in 27 minutes -- 6.5k/s
idle, 20.5k/s under load -- with the threaded handler burning 22% of a
core continuously from boot.

The vendor's pull-up is not wrong downstream, where the nq-nci driver
drives VEN high from its own probe and the controller owns the line; there
the bias only picks an idle level.  It is wrong here.  Pull it down: the
controller drives push-pull once powered, so an active interrupt still
reaches the CPU, and an unpowered controller now reads as idle.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/msm8998-google-taimen.dts | 12 +++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/msm8998-google-taimen.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/msm8998-google-taimen.dts
index e4bae0859c11..8fb5e1239fae 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/msm8998-google-taimen.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/msm8998-google-taimen.dts
@@ -561,12 +561,22 @@ ts_i2c_switch: ts-i2c-switch {
 	 * nfc_enable_active). The vendor groups gpio116 -- the eSE enable --
 	 * into nfc_enable_active; it is left out here because the eSE is not
 	 * described and driving its enable line would be a side effect.
+	 *
+	 * The INT bias is deliberately NOT the vendor's. Downstream drives VEN
+	 * high from its own probe, so the controller owns gpio92 and the
+	 * vendor's pull-up only picks the idle level. Mainline's nxp-nci keeps
+	 * VEN low until userspace calls dev_up(), so a pull-up holds the line
+	 * high against a tri-stated controller and the LEVEL_HIGH irq is
+	 * asserted from request_threaded_irq() onwards -- 6k/s idle, 20k/s
+	 * under load, forever, because the handler's I2C read NACKs
+	 * (-EREMOTEIO), latches hard_fault and then acks nothing. The
+	 * controller drives push-pull once powered, so pull it down.
 	 */
 	nfc_int_active: nfc-int-active {
 		pins = "gpio92";
 		function = "gpio";
 		drive-strength = <6>;
-		bias-pull-up;
+		bias-pull-down;
 	};
 
 	nfc_enable_active: nfc-enable-active {
